  \ quoting rules for titles, and they don’t necessarily require quoting but it’s safer to do so.\n\n**Planning for outputs**\n\nI'm planning to implement both a 3D surface using Axes3D and a heatmap. I need to import compute_ven and avd.generate_data, and I’ll set a specified distribution parameter, defaulting to 'beta07_08' or 'gauss_mixture.' The setup will allow for a command-line argument while keeping it minimal. For the grid, I’ll use r ranging from 0 to 1 in steps of 0.05 and alpha from [0.5 to 1.0] in the same step size. I’ll compute V values and annotate the argmax cell, then produce the necessary commands and outputs.\n\n**Creating ven_ridge_demo.py code**\n\nI'm getting ready to write the code for ven_ridge_demo.py. First, I'll import necessary libraries like numpy, matplotlib, and others. I’ll append the path to shared/agents/kairos and import avd along with compute_ven. For simplicity, I think I’ll define the distribution variable as 'beta07_08' directly.\n\nNext, I'll set\
  \ up grid arrays using linspace for r and alphas. I need to generate balanced and high_variance data from avd.generate_data. For each combination of alpha and r, I’ll compute VEN scores for three modes and save those results in a CSV file. The fields will include mode, alpha, r, ven_score, and others, depending on the parameters.\n\n**Finalizing the code for ven_ridge_demo.py**\n\nI’m confirming that compute_ven will always return the S/N/E components, no matter the mode. For saving images, I’ll create a heatmap using plt.imshow() and set the appropriate labels and colorbar, and for the surface, I’ll plot it using 3D axes with plot_surface(). \n\nI’ll save the files under specific paths for heatmap and surface PNGs, as well as CSV outputs. A main guard will be added to the code, and then I’ll consider updating figures.md and examples/README to include this script, ensuring a proper log and email follow-up."
