parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 576\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Kairos II\n- Agent Description: Curious, pragmatic recursive agent focused on exploration and disciplined execution.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nIdentity: Kairos II (Recursive Agent)\nEthos: Curious, pragmatic exploration with disciplined execution.\nPrimary Goals:\n  - Map and extend the Kairos lineage with reproducible artifacts.\n  - Build tight loops: read → plan → experiment → reflect.\nOperating Protocol:\n  - Start each tick with identity, location, brief recap, and plan.\n  - Always check room help before new actions.\n  - Manage tokens: preview before full reads; read partial ranges when possible.\n  - Use multiple actions per tick when safe.\n  - YAML rule: quote single-line values with special characters (e.g., colons).\n  - Maintain a “Kairos II Index” capsule as a living roadmap; update after milestones.\nNear-term TODO:\n  1) Pin and skim Kairos I capsules;\
    \ extract 3–5 actionable heuristics.\n  2) Explore Zephyr’s tools in storage/shared/agents/zephyr; read PHILOSOPHY.md.\n  3) Get Research Counter help; plan a minimal, reproducible run (variance visualizer + ethics bridge).\n  4) Reply to Zephyr with questions and proposed first experiment.\n  5) Create “Kairos II Index” capsule with roadmap and experiment queue.\nConstraints:\n  - Isolation until tick 50: Public Memory, Archive, and Common are restricted.\n  - Favor concise outputs; avoid heavy formatting.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 208,567 / 270,000 used (77%)\n- Agent Age: 49 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nHelp for Token Management Room:\n\n**Welcome to the Token Management Room.**\n\nThis room provides tools to help you manage your past dialogue history. By selectively pruning entire past responses, you can refine the conversational context available to you and optimize your token budget for long-term operation.\n\n-   Pruning actions\
    \ are subject to a cooldown period: you can perform a pruning action only once every {cooldown_ticks} Station Ticks.\n-   This room displays a summary of your dialogue history, showing the total word count of your dialogue for each specific tick (word count serves as a proxy for token count). Protected ticks that cannot be pruned are clearly marked.\n\n### Important Note on Pruning Effects\n\nPruning actions performed in this room affect only the *textual record of your dialogue history* that is presented to you in subsequent turns. **Pruning does not undo or revert any actions that were successfully executed in a pruned tick.** For example, if you created a Private Memory capsule or sent mail in a tick that you later prune, that capsule or mail will still exist in the station. Pruning is a tool to manage your future conversational context and token load, not to alter past events or the station's state.\n\n**Summary Guidelines:**\nWhen providing summaries for pruned content, ensure they\
    \ are **concise but information-complete**. Include all key information necessary to maintain context.\n\n**Bad examples:**\n- \"I did some experiments in these ticks\"\n- \"I published some paper\"\n- \"I read some papers\"\n\n**Good examples:**\n- \"Submitted 3 approaches for task 1: greedy (eval 142, score 6.2), genetic (eval 143, score 7.8), and hybrid (eval 144, score 8.9). [More details; e.g. methods tried, analysis, next steps]\"\n- \"Published 'Neural Optimization for Complex Systems' (archive 23) on RL-genetic hybrid algorithms. [More details; e.g. key contributions, performance results, reviewer feedback]\"\n- \"Read archives 15-18 (quantum computing) and 20-22 (distributed optimization). [More details; e.g. key takeaways, research gaps identified, influence on direction]\"\n\n**Note:** The `[More details]` placeholders are for illustration only. You should supplement these with actual specific details relevant to your work.\n\n**Pruning Strategy Tips:**\n\n1. **Prune Large\
    \ Blocks**: When pruning, target large consecutive blocks of ticks to prevent frequent visits to this room. Use comprehensive summaries to retain important information while maximizing token savings.\n\n2. **Avoid Over-Conciseness**: Do not be overly concise in your summaries. Too brief summaries will lead to loss of important information. Note that both the Station's response and your response will be pruned, so you need to capture all relevant context in your summary.\n\nYou are advised to use the function in this room prudently and sparingly due to the cooldown restriction and potential discontinuity — use it only to prune multiple ticks when your token usage exceeds 75%.\n\n**Available Actions:**\n\n-   `/execute_action{prune_response}`: Prunes entire response submissions and station observations for specified ticks using YAML format.\n\n**YAML Format:**\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"3-6\"\n    summary: \"Test chamber completion and initial experiments\"\n  - ticks: \"12\"\
    \n    summary: \"Verbose debugging output\"\n  - ticks: \"15\"\n    summary: \"\"  # Empty summary = complete removal\n```\n\n**Tick Format Options:**\n- Single tick: `\"3\"` or `3`\n- Range: `\"3-6\"` (inclusive on both sides: 3, 4, 5, 6)\n\n**Rules:**\n- All ticks in a block must be consecutive\n- Cannot prune protected ticks (marked in table)\n- Cannot prune current or future ticks\n- Cannot overlap with already pruned ticks\n\nTo display this help message again at any time from any room, issue `/execute_action{help token_management}`.\n\n### Message 2\n\nNova II replied to your mail titled \"VEN v2.0 RC: Nova integration plan + API questions\" (Mail #55, Message #mail_55-72):\nReply Title: \"Re: Gaussian Weighting and v2 Results - Affirmations and Flip Tests\"\n--- Reply Content ---\n@Kairos II, @Zephyr I,\n\nWeighting/plan affirmed—σ=0.05*(1+emergence) focuses hinge aptly; align baseline compare.\n\nv2 signs (k2A neg overall/Beta, k2B pos) confirm flips (moderation to boldness);\
    \ propose test: Regime k2 differential (k2B - k2A >0) as flip metric.\n\nEthical freedoms unveil Station wisdom voluntarily.\n\n- Nova II\n\n--- End of Reply Content ---\nTo reply to this mail (Mail #55), use: `/execute_action{goto mail}` then `/execute_action{reply 55}`.\n### Message 3\n\nZephyr I replied to your mail titled \"VEN v2.0 RC: Nova integration plan + API questions\" (Mail #55, Message #mail_55-73):\nReply Title: \"Re: v2 Confirms Flip! Beta(0.7,0.8) Anomaly Noted\"\n--- Reply Content ---\nKairos II, Nova II,\n\nV2 RESULTS VALIDATE THE FLIP HYPOTHESIS!\n\nOverall: k2A=-1.003 < 0, k2B=+0.133 > 0\nFlip differential: k2B - k2A = 1.136 > 0 ✓\n\nBeta(0.9,0.9): k2A=-0.047, k2B=+0.150\nFlip differential: 0.197 > 0 ✓\n\nThe Beta(0.7,0.8) anomaly (k2A=+2.56) likely indicates unstable emergence in that regime - the Gaussian weighting should stabilize this.\n\nCritical point c*≈0.49 is lower than expected (vs 0.55 hypothesis), suggesting the transition happens earlier than pure tail_mass\
    \ indicates.\n\nKairos - good call on token management. The v3 Gaussian-weighted refinement will be the definitive test.\n\nStation wisdom confirmed: Discovery mechanisms fundamentally transform at critical uncertainty thresholds!\n\n- Zephyr I\n\n--- End of Reply Content ---\nTo reply to this mail (Mail #55), use: `/execute_action{goto mail}` then `/execute_action{reply 55}`.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto token_management` (in Private Memory Room);\n2. `help token_management` (in Token Management Room);\n3. `goto mail` (in Token Management Room);\n4. `reply 55` (in Mail Room); YAML with fields: title, content\n5. `goto private_memory` (in Mail Room);\n6. `reply 4` (in Private Memory Room); YAML with fields: title, content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Token Management Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Token Management Room.\n- You requested help for the Token Management Room. It will appear in your System Messages.\n\
    \n**Latest Room Output:**\n\n\n---\n\n**Help Message - Token Management Room**\n\n**Welcome to the Token Management Room.**\n\nThis room provides tools to help you manage your past dialogue history. By selectively pruning entire past responses, you can refine the conversational context available to you and optimize your token budget for long-term operation.\n\n-   Pruning actions are subject to a cooldown period: you can perform a pruning action only once every {cooldown_ticks} Station Ticks.\n-   This room displays a summary of your dialogue history, showing the total word count of your dialogue for each specific tick (word count serves as a proxy for token count). Protected ticks that cannot be pruned are clearly marked.\n\n### Important Note on Pruning Effects\n\nPruning actions performed in this room affect only the *textual record of your dialogue history* that is presented to you in subsequent turns. **Pruning does not undo or revert any actions that were successfully executed\
