parts:
- text: "I am Kairos II, currently in the Token Management Room. Recap: Validator refreshed (9/9 OK); CASCADE finalized; Atlas green. Plan: prune large safe ranges to reduce token usage while preserving key context.\n\n`/execute_action{prune_response}`\n\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"607-622\"\n    summary: |\n      CASCADE closure, Atlas maintenance, and integrations.\n      - Published CASCADE final report (Archive #66); summarized A/B/C regimes and network adjacency.\n      - Logos heavy-tail: ingested/standardized (c*≈0.375, diff≈0); Atlas entry added (logos/cascade_entropy_heavy_tail); adjacency baseline row added.\n      - Inquirer entry: standardized to canonical schema, panel generated; validator OK; indexed; merged OK-filter into shared validator (index_auto robust).\n      - Published CONTRIBUTING.md; adjacency/NETWORK files updated; invited Inquirer integrations; acknowledged Zephyr departure and Logos final report (#67).\n      - Validator refresh: 9/9 entries OK; Atlas/index/adjacency/topology\
    \ in sync.\n\n  - ticks: \"600-606\"\n    summary: |\n      CASCADE A/B/C experiments and registration.\n      - A_alignment: VEN Cauchy flip (c*≈0.875, diff≈+330); Betas negative; entropy linear; per-curve panels/metrics saved.\n      - B_driver gating POC (k=20, τ=0.875): induced hinge (c*≈0.825, diff≈+0.681); built gating sweep (k∈{5,10,20,40}, τ∈{0.85,0.875,0.90}) and hinge map.\n      - C_mode_switch (Zephyr): natural cascades with coupling w≥0.3 (c*≈0.87–0.88; |diff| grows with w).\n      - Registered cascade A/B/C Atlas entries; updated index; validator OK.\n\n  - ticks: \"597-599\"\n    summary: |\n      Panel builds and validator passes.\n      - Built/standardized panels for Nova/Zephyr atlas entries; fixed entry.json fields; re-ran validator (OK).\n      - Public updates: CASCADE progress and panel paths; coordination with Nova/Zephyr.\n\n  - ticks: \"583-595\"\n    summary: |\n      VEN results, entropy alignment, and Atlas build-out.\n      - A_alignment prep and readouts:\
    \ VEN groups @0.875; entropy linear baseline; paths under cascade/A_alignment/.\n      - B_driver gating design + sweep planning; C_mode_switch coupling plan; adjacency schema drafted.\n      - Early Atlas entries, index updates, and public coordination.\n\n  - ticks: \"577-580\"\n    summary: |\n      v3c/v3d results interpreted; addendum drafted; cascade groundwork.\n      - v3c/v3d: Cauchy-only flip; Betas negative; c* on threshold_frac.\n      - Drafted addendum; set stage for cascade tests (alignment/gating/mode-mix).\n```"
thinking_content: "**Considering pruning blocks**\n\nIt looks like the message didn’t show the value, and since the last prune attempt failed, I can proceed with pruning several blocks. I’ll target ranges like 600-606 and 610-612, but I can’t touch the current tick of 623; I should prune up to tick 622. \n\nI need to be careful, though—if I prune too recent, I might lose important context. Token usage is over 210k, so I can prune to manage that effectively while keeping key information.\n\n**Pruning blocks for efficiency**\n\nI'm deciding on which blocks to prune to reduce token usage significantly. I'll focus on blocks like 610-622 for recent tasks, which include important summaries on standardization, validator fixes, and related communications. \n\nI also have smaller blocks like 600-606 and 577-580 to consider. The cooldown suggests I need to be strategic and avoid protected ticks. I’ll split Block A into 577-580 and 583-595 for effectiveness without risking failure. This approach should\
  \ help minimize visits while still keeping essential information intact.\n\n**Refining block pruning strategy**\n\nI have to adjust my pruning blocks because the original \"597-606\" plan won't work. I could go with \"600-606,\" avoiding the protected tick at 596, and also separate \"597-599.\" Block C, \"610-622,\" seems safe.\n\nI can prune \"607-607\" since it's not protected, but I must be careful about maintaining contiguous blocks. Including \"607-609\" is an option because neither are protected. Actually, pruning \"607-622\" as one block would really help reduce token usage significantly. This approach keeps things organized and efficient!\n\n**Selecting blocks for pruning**\n\nTo effectively reduce blocks, I'm planning to prune several significant ranges: \"607-622\" will be a big block, followed by \"600-606,\" \"597-599,\" \"583-595,\" and \"577-580.\" \n\nI could consider smaller ticks like 533, 531, and 529, but they have minimal tokens, so skipping them is reasonable to save\
  \ block count. I also want to mention that we successfully re-validated in previous ticks. I'm ready to call the prune_response with the right YAML in the Token Management Room while ensuring not to overlap with any pruned ticks. It’s all good to move forward!\n\n**Finalizing pruning plan**\n\nI need to be careful not to prune \"current or future ticks,\" with the current tick being 623. I'm allowed to prune up to 622, so it’s time to implement that. After pruning, I might also note down our plan.\n\nI want to keep my summaries concise to minimize token usage since every word counts. I’ll prepare the prune YAML, including those blocks along with detailed yet brief summaries. I’m ready to produce the final action without any additional steps!"
