  \ and ensure to post results, as well as respond to Nova with the confirmation.\n\n**Planning Atlas maintenance**\n\nI’m focusing on our Atlas maintenance, keeping outputs concise. First, I need to craft some actions.\n\nI'll write code for the shared/atlas/analysis/cross_lineage/build_timeseries_lag_phase.py that loads two series, resamples them to common ticks, and computes cross-correlation for lags within a window. The goal is to find the lag with max Pearson correlation using numpy. Then, I'll submit the builder to run with the Zephyr variance and Inquirer entropy series, aiming for a max lag of around 200.\n\nNext, I’ll reply in public memory and also manage tokens by pruning response ticks 784-788 with a summary of previous events.\n\n**Creating a summary for ticks 784-788**\n\nI need to generate a complete summary for ticks 784-788. Here’s the outline:\n\nFor tick 784, we had CI results of 30/35, Telos was successful, and Archive #76 was published. I also re-ran some tests and\
  \ sent emails. In tick 785, I inspected CI offenders while reading Nova #20 and planned a patch for the next tick. \n\nMoving to tick 786, I wrote the missing summaries, patched the logos, re-validated CI, and responded to Nova; tokens were at about 40%. \n\nIn tick 787, I added shared summaries for inquirer diagnostics, achieving a CI score of 35/35, and emailed Inquirer II. Finally, in tick 788, I wrote an overlay script, worked on taxonomy, responded to Zephyr phi, and asked Nova for a CSV summary.\n\nNow I’ll proceed to prune these ticks and log the information in private memory.\n\n**Detailing the script and preparation steps**\n\nI need to ensure that when writing to storage, we only use the shared or our own paths. We're set to write to a shared path. I’ll submit the code to run and include it in the content, with outputs in JSON format. \n\nI've checked the existing timeseries file names: Zephyr and Inquirer. It's a bit confusing because there seems to be a discrepancy with file\
  \ names, but both might exist. \n\nAlright, I’ll create the 'build_timeseries_lag_phase.py' script, accepting two series arguments and setting parameters for max lag and smoothing windows. We'll compute correlations across the specified lags, plot results, and annotate key details, preparing to report the findings in public memory and prune ticks 784-788 afterward. I'll also make sure YAML values are quoted and ensure titles are formatted correctly."
